[
 {:seon.task/name "a-commit-of-one-datom-costs-its-nodes"
  :seon.task/plan "Where it stands on main's pins (Datahike 8250357f, which carries the node addresses of one digest, a leaf's address taken from the leaf as stored, the parent's commit-graph entry not read back, and the out-of-line pass that reads the store's collection generation once a flush and walks only collection values; docs/forks.md#datahike). Measured 2026-11-10/11 by Deep fixes' helpers (notes honest-write-phases and nodes-per-write, 2026-11-11). Direct ``d/transact`` of one datom on a 28.7k-datom LMDB store (history, content-addressed nodes, the :sync writer, median of 31): 171k ticks, 787 KB, 6.4 nodes, 9.4 keys, 0.93 ms; designed about 150k. Through root's writer (main of 250-286k datoms, 40 writes): 23-26 nodes and 26-29 keys a write (27 on the standing root, whose history trees are a level deeper), 53 KB of zstd values, ``datahike.writer/transact-batch!`` 597-614k ticks and 3.2 MB, ``seon.system/transact-main!``'s deref p50 5.2-6.0 ms; of the write: the apply about 80k; the flush about 190-210k (out-of-line 89-110k, address hashing about 100k); the commit id 28k; konserve's multi-assoc about 330k (serializing each node and meta about 120k, store keys about 54k, the 27 meta maps (each key's own :key, :last-write and :revision, 144 B: not a constant, as this entry first said; their cost is not measured apart), zstd 26k, the LMDB puts 27k); the LMDB commit about 0.35 ms of waiting; about 0.4 ms of thread hops; the sync (F_FULLFSYNC, 3.7-7 ms) is the flusher's, off the answer's path. Why 25 nodes for one datom: 13 are the transaction entity's (``:db/txInstant`` 6, `:seon.tx/runtime` 1, `:seon.tx/branch` 5 with its avet path, sharing an aevt branch), 4-6 the history trees', 8 the floor (one root-to-leaf path in eavt and aevt). A commit costs about 112k ticks fixed and 18.2k a node. Since measured, `:seon.tx/branch` is no longer indexed by value and `:seon.system/collected` keeps no history (docs/storage.md#what-a-datom-costs-to-add), which the same measure puts at 5 and 4 nodes fewer on that write: not measured again on main. Group commit shares the transaction entity's nodes: ten writes in one commit wrote 31 nodes, not 250, 8x fewer commit ticks. A larger branching factor costs more (128: 5 fewer nodes, +21% bytes, +7% ticks at 250k datoms; 64 is the cheapest of 64-512 at every size measured). Left, largest first: the store write (tasks konserve.impl.defaults/a-value-already-serialized-is-stored-as-it-is and konserve.impl.defaults/a-store-key-costs-one-digest, whose first two changes are on konserve 1119607: 353.8k to 335.3k ticks on a direct one-datom commit; what it leaves is the digest shim in jolt.crypto, 938 ticks a digest against 353); the address hashing (one native digest of a node's bytes, inferred 35-40k); the commit id (28k: hasch of the stored commit; the audit recomputes it from content, so it does not change without the audit); the commit record's ``:cache-revisions`` (90% of its 4.3 KB, 14k ticks a commit). The owner's decisions, not small fixes: dropping a tx-meta datom (facts Seon records), and whether content ids that scatter new entities across every index are worth the pages they dirty. Proof when done: a cost proof of a one-datom commit by nodes written, on a store on disk, sizes by store depth."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "atoms-deps"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps. The owner, 2026-11-12: an atom is a proc not yet made (AGENTS.md §3 step 4). Inventory of seon.merge and the forks' Seon-facing code, read 2026-11-12 13:20, each classified (a local accumulator becomes a value; a running thing's state becomes a proc's state; a handoff becomes a channel; in Jolt, a language primitive as Clojure's runtime has it stays): seon.merge holds none. (1) datahike.writer SyncWriter's last-cid, known-generation and pending volatiles (writer.cljc create-writer :sync; follow-head! and transact-batch! write them from outside the writer): a running writer's state; it becomes the state of the one proc that commits a branch (root's :writer proc, an agent's commit), passed in and returned, never reached into. (2) datahike.connections/*connections* and each Connection's atom: upstream's API (d/connect answers an IAtom; the registry is process-wide running state); Seon reaches the registry only through force-branch! (api/impl.cljc, connector/follow-head!). Decide with Data model whether Seon's connections move behind the writer proc (the proc holds the db value; d/connect stays for upstream's tests) or stay as upstream's. (3) datahike.connector/-connect-impl* resources volatile: a local accumulator; becomes a value threaded through the connect. (4) konserve.jolt.lmdb envs and holders atoms and each env's :open? atom: a running resource owner's state (env lifetime, holder counts); becomes one proc's state that opens, holds and closes envs, its callers asking it on a channel. (5) persistent-sorted-set's transient edit flag (volatile! true in asTransient): a language primitive, as Clojure's own transients keep an edit reference; stays. (6) datahike.writer LocalWriter's inflight-ops atom: upstream's async writer, which Seon does not run; stays with upstream. Proof when done: the sweep's check names no atom or volatile in these namespaces outside (5) and (6), and each moved state's proc is driven through the step-fn contract (docs/flow.md#the-step-fn-contract-every-proc-follows)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/the-sync-writer-state-is-its-procs-state}}
]
